add("IT / General and application controls", "cobit", r"""
IT general controls|What are ITGC controls?|IT general controls support the reliable and authorized operation of systems, commonly across access, changes, and operations.|Their financial-reporting relevance depends on the applications, data, and automated controls relying on them. A failed ITGC requires impact analysis rather than an automatic conclusion that every balance is wrong.|Restricted deployment access helps preserve an ERP's invoice approval configuration.|Map ITGC coverage to specific applications, reports, interfaces, and automated controls.|Treating all IT controls as SOX key controls or all ITGC failures as material weaknesses.|System inventories, access records, change tickets, and dependency maps.|Identify which relied-upon processing or information could be affected by the weakness.|Application controls;IT dependencies;ICFR
User access provisioning|What should a new-user access control check?|Provisioning should establish a valid identity, approved business need, and permissions appropriate to the role.|Approval must precede access when the design requires it, and role selection should consider conflicts and sensitive capabilities.|A new accountant receives posting rights but not vendor-bank maintenance or payment release.|Use role-based access and check conflicts before enabling sensitive privileges.|Treating a manager's generic approval as authorization for every requested privilege.|Access requests, approvals, role definitions, and actual system assignments.|Compare granted permissions with approved business need and check unauthorized additions.|Least privilege;Segregation of duties;Access reviews
User termination access|How quickly should access be removed when someone leaves?|Removal timing should follow a risk-based approved policy and applicable obligations; SOX does not impose one universal number of hours.|High-risk or involuntary departures may require immediate coordinated removal. Coverage should include applications, identity providers, sessions, and privileged access.|HR notifies IT of a departure and the team disables ERP access and active remote sessions.|Reconcile departure populations to disabled accounts and investigate delays.|Checking only the network account while leaving local application access active.|HR departure records, disable timestamps, session revocation, and reconciliation results.|Compare the complete departure population with actual access removal across in-scope systems.|Joiner mover leaver;Privileged access;HR interfaces
Access recertification|How should a user access review be performed?|A knowledgeable reviewer checks whether current access remains appropriate and resolves unnecessary or conflicting permissions.|The population must be complete, include sensitive nonhuman accounts where relevant, and show what reviewers actually evaluated.|A finance owner challenges a former accounts-payable employee's retained payment rights after a transfer.|Provide business-readable permission descriptions and track removals to completion.|Approving a user list without understanding privileges or confirming changes were made.|Complete access extracts, reviewer decisions, conflict analysis, and removal evidence.|Reperform selected entitlement decisions and trace rejected access to revocation.|Least privilege;Mover controls;Access provisioning
Privileged access|Why are administrator accounts a major audit concern?|Privileged accounts may bypass application safeguards, alter data, or change controls and logs.|Risk depends on capabilities and oversight, including service accounts, emergency access, and database-level rights.|A database administrator could update financial tables outside the application approval workflow.|Limit standing privilege, protect credentials, and independently review sensitive activity.|Ignoring service accounts or assuming named-user reviews cover all administrative access.|Privileged inventories, vault records, session logs, and independent reviews.|Determine whether a privileged user can alter reporting data without timely independent detection.|Management override;Database security;Emergency access
Segregation of duties|What is segregation of duties?|Segregation of duties separates incompatible responsibilities so one person cannot complete and conceal a harmful transaction alone.|Evaluate effective capabilities across systems and manual processes, not just job titles. Where separation is impractical, assess precise compensating controls.|One person creates suppliers while another independently approves bank changes and releases payments.|Define conflicts around realistic misuse scenarios and review combined access.|Assuming two job titles prove separation when both users share credentials.|Role matrices, cross-system entitlements, conflict reports, and mitigation evidence.|Trace whether one individual can initiate, authorize, record, and conceal the same transaction.|Fraud risk;Compensating controls;Least privilege
Change management|What should auditors check in IT change management?|Check that changes are authorized, tested, appropriately segregated, deployed accurately, and traceable.|Include code, configuration, interfaces, reports, and emergency changes that affect relied-upon processing. A ticket number alone is weak evidence.|A tax calculation change is tested against expected results before independent deployment.|Link requirements, test results, approvals, and the actual production version.|Approving a ticket after deployment without assessing whether the required preventive control failed.|Change population, approvals, test evidence, deployment logs, and version records.|Trace selected production changes backward to authorization and forward to validated outcomes.|ITGC;SDLC;Emergency changes
Emergency changes|Can emergency IT changes bypass normal approvals?|An emergency process may use different authorized safeguards, but urgency should not mean uncontrolled changes.|Define emergency criteria, restricted privileges, testing feasible before release, and prompt independent review afterward.|A critical payment outage is fixed under emergency authority and independently reviewed with deployment evidence.|Track emergency usage and investigate repeated claims of urgency.|Using emergency tickets as a routine way to avoid segregation or testing.|Incident linkage, emergency authorization, deployed version, and retrospective review.|Check whether the change met emergency criteria and whether required follow-up occurred.|Change management;Incident response;Privileged access
IT operations|What IT operations controls matter for financial reporting?|Relevant operations controls support complete, accurate, timely processing and recoverability of reporting information.|Batch scheduling, job failure handling, interfaces, backups, and incident escalation matter when they affect financial data or reporting deadlines.|A failed overnight interface is investigated and reconciled before the ledger close.|Connect operational alerts to business impact and accountable resolution.|Closing job alerts without confirming missing transactions were processed correctly.|Job logs, incident tickets, reconciliations, recovery records, and escalation.|Trace a failed job from detection through correct reprocessing without duplication.|Interfaces;Backups;Financial close
Application controls|What is an application control?|An application control is a programmed or application-supported check over transactions or data within a business process.|Examples include matching, validations, calculations, and approval routing. Reliability depends on configuration, inputs, and relevant ITGCs.|An ERP rejects an invoice when its price exceeds the approved purchase order tolerance.|Test approved configuration and both valid and invalid transaction paths.|Assuming automation means no testing is needed.|Configuration, test cases, input data, outputs, and change/access support.|Reperform the relevant logic and investigate exceptions, overrides, and untested paths.|ITGC;Automated controls;Three-way match
Interfaces|How do we control data moving between systems?|Interface controls establish that data transfers are complete, accurate, authorized, and handled appropriately when rejected.|Counts alone may miss altered values; totals alone may hide offsetting errors. Reprocessing needs controls against duplication.|A payroll interface reconciles employee counts and monetary totals and separately resolves rejected records.|Use source-to-target reconciliation and controlled error reprocessing.|Treating a successful technical transmission message as proof that every record posted correctly.|Source and destination totals, error queues, mapping rules, and reprocessing logs.|Trace normal, rejected, and retried records across the interface.|Data lineage;Completeness;IT operations
Spreadsheet controls|How should important financial spreadsheets be controlled?|Apply controls proportionate to the spreadsheet's reporting importance, complexity, and change risk.|Protect formulas and access, validate inputs and logic, manage versions, and retain evidence of review. Password protection alone does not prove calculation accuracy.|A reserve model locks formulas, reconciles input balances, and receives an independent reasonableness review.|Inventory important spreadsheets and test changes to formulas and external links.|Reviewing only the final total or emailing uncontrolled versions between preparer and reviewer.|Version history, formula tests, input reconciliations, and reviewer comments.|Recalculate sensitive formulas and check hidden cells, links, and manual overrides.|End-user computing;IPE;Accounting estimates
Information produced by the entity|What is IPE, and why do auditors test it?|IPE is information produced by the entity that is used in controls or audit procedures; its reliability must fit the intended use.|Relevant work may address completeness, accuracy, parameters, logic, and the level of detail. Not every report needs identical testing.|An access-review report is checked for excluded inactive-looking accounts that still retain usable privileges.|Identify the precise report version and parameters used in the control.|Assuming an ERP report is reliable simply because it is standard or system generated.|Report logic, parameters, source reconciliations, access/change controls, and field tests.|Validate the attributes the control actually relies on and assess omitted records.|Audit evidence;Report validation;Management review
Service organization reports|Can a SOC report replace our own control testing?|A relevant SOC report can provide evidence about service-organization controls, but it does not cover all user-entity responsibilities.|SOC 1 concerns controls relevant to user entities' financial reporting; SOC 2 addresses trust services criteria. Type 1 addresses design at a date; Type 2 also covers operating effectiveness over a period.|A payroll customer reviews a SOC 1 Type 2 report and tests its own complementary user controls.|Check scope, period, opinion, exceptions, subservice organizations, and user responsibilities.|Treating SOC 2 as automatically sufficient for ICFR or accepting a report without reading exceptions.|The complete report, relevance assessment, gap-period evidence, and user-control tests.|Determine whether the report supports the specific outsourced process and reporting period.|CUECs;Outsourcing;Third-party risk
Complementary user entity controls|What are CUECs in a SOC report?|CUECs are controls the service organization assumes user entities implement to support the relevant control objectives.|The customer must assess which listed controls apply and whether they operate. A clean service-provider report does not prove the customer's controls work.|A payroll provider assumes the customer authorizes employee changes before submission.|Map applicable CUECs to named internal controls and retain operating evidence.|Marking every CUEC not applicable without analyzing the service arrangement.|SOC report mapping, customer process documentation, and control evidence.|Trace a provider assumption to a functioning customer control.|SOC 1;User responsibilities;Outsourcing
Cloud controls|Who is responsible for controls when finance systems run in the cloud?|Responsibility is shared according to the service model and contract, while the customer retains accountability for its reporting and risk decisions.|Provider assurance may cover infrastructure but leave customer configuration, access, integrations, and data use outside that coverage.|A SaaS provider protects its platform while the customer reviews finance-user privileges and approval configuration.|Document the actual responsibility boundary and update it when services change.|Assuming a cloud provider takes over all SOX accountability.|Contracts, responsibility matrices, assurance reports, and customer configuration reviews.|Identify any control gap between provider scope and customer operation.|SOC reports;Shared responsibility;Access controls
Cybersecurity and SOX|Is every cybersecurity issue a SOX issue?|No. Evaluate whether the issue affects financial reporting controls, data integrity, or related disclosure responsibilities.|A cyber incident may have significant business or disclosure implications without being an ICFR material weakness; a reporting-system compromise may affect both.|Compromised administrator credentials prompt assessment of ledger changes, access controls, and disclosure escalation.|Connect incident facts to affected reporting processes and preserve evidence.|Automatically equating a breach with a material weakness or excluding cyber incidents from reporting assessment.|Incident scope, affected-system map, data-integrity checks, and disclosure decisions.|Assess what financial data or controls could have been altered and whether reliable evidence remains.|ICFR;Incident response;Disclosure controls
""")

add("Financial reporting / Business processes", "icfr", r"""
Revenue controls|What controls help prevent incorrect revenue recognition?|Controls should address contract terms, performance evidence, pricing, cutoff, estimates, and required disclosures under the applicable accounting framework.|The relevant risks differ for subscriptions, goods, services, and complex arrangements; an invoice alone may not establish earned revenue.|A period-end review compares shipment and acceptance evidence with recorded revenue and investigates side agreements.|Link controls to specific recognition criteria and unusual terms.|Assuming cash receipt or invoice creation always proves revenue should be recognized.|Contracts, fulfillment evidence, cutoff reports, and accounting reviews.|Test transactions around period-end and challenge unusual arrangements against applicable policy.|Revenue recognition;Cutoff;Fraud risk
Procure-to-pay controls|What are the main risks and controls in procure-to-pay?|Key risks include unauthorized purchases, fictitious suppliers, duplicate invoices, incorrect recording, and improper payments.|Controls often combine supplier validation, purchasing authority, invoice matching, payment review, and reconciliations.|A duplicate invoice alert prevents paying the same supplier invoice twice after a formatting change.|Protect supplier bank changes independently from routine invoice approval.|Assuming three-way matching prevents fraud when supplier setup and payment rights are uncontrolled.|Supplier changes, purchase approvals, receipts, invoice matches, and payment logs.|Trace a payment end to end and assess how unauthorized or duplicate items could pass.|Vendor master;Three-way match;Segregation of duties
Bank reconciliations|What makes a bank reconciliation an effective control?|It compares bank activity and balances with accounting records and resolves differences promptly.|Completeness of accounts, accurate source data, independent review, and follow-up of old or unusual items are essential.|A reconciliation identifies an unrecorded bank fee and a suspicious transfer requiring investigation.|Maintain a complete bank-account inventory and age unresolved differences.|Signing a reconciliation while unexplained reconciling items accumulate.|Bank statements, ledger extracts, reconciliation schedules, and resolution support.|Reperform the reconciliation and inspect significant or aged differences through closure.|Cash controls;Detective controls;Completeness
Journal entry controls|How should companies control manual journal entries?|Journal controls should address authorization, support, appropriate access, accurate posting, and unusual or override activity.|High-risk entries may occur after normal close, through interfaces, or directly in the ledger, so coverage must extend beyond the standard approval screen.|A late manual revenue entry requires documented business rationale and independent review of support.|Reconcile the entry population and focus reviews on meaningful risk indicators.|Assuming approved entries cannot be fraudulent or ignoring entries posted by administrators.|Complete entry extracts, support, approvals, access logs, and unusual-entry reviews.|Investigate unusual users, timing, accounts, descriptions, and unsupported reversals.|Management override;Fraud risk;Financial close
Inventory controls|What controls are important over inventory?|Inventory controls address existence, completeness, ownership, costing, cutoff, and obsolescence as relevant.|Physical counts support quantity but do not by themselves establish ownership or appropriate valuation.|A warehouse counts stock while finance separately reviews slow-moving items for valuation adjustments.|Control count movements, investigate differences, and align costing reviews with the accounting policy.|Using a successful count to conclude that valuation and rights assertions are satisfied.|Count records, movement logs, reconciliations, costing data, and reserve reviews.|Trace items in both directions and separately assess valuation and ownership evidence.|Assertions;Cycle counts;Inventory reserves
Payroll controls|How do auditors evaluate payroll controls?|Evaluate authorized employees and pay changes, accurate calculations, valid payments, and reconciliation to accounting records.|HR, payroll, and banking responsibilities should be coordinated without letting one person create and pay fictitious employees unnoticed.|An independent review compares payroll changes with approved HR actions and investigates duplicate bank accounts.|Reconcile HR populations, payroll output, and payment files before release.|Reviewing only total payroll cost while unauthorized employee changes remain hidden.|HR actions, change logs, payroll calculations, payment approvals, and reconciliations.|Trace hires, terminations, and pay changes through payroll and bank output.|Ghost employees;Interfaces;Segregation of duties
Financial close|What should a strong financial close control process include?|A strong close coordinates reconciliations, journal reviews, estimates, consolidation, disclosures, and issue escalation.|A checklist supports completeness but must be backed by substantive review and resolution of significant accounting questions.|The controller reviews unresolved account reconciliations before approving consolidation rather than simply checking completion dates.|Assign dependencies and review criteria and escalate unresolved material matters before reporting.|Equating a fast close or completed checklist with accurate reporting.|Close calendar, reconciliation status, review evidence, accounting memos, and issue decisions.|Trace significant balances and unresolved items into the reporting approval process.|Record-to-report;Journal entries;Disclosure controls
Accounting estimates|How do controls over accounting estimates work?|They support appropriate methods, reliable data, reasonable assumptions, and review of estimation uncertainty and bias.|A reviewer needs enough expertise and precision to challenge management's model rather than merely approve its output.|A warranty reserve review compares claims trends, product changes, and prior estimate accuracy.|Document sensitivity, contrary evidence, and the rationale for significant assumptions.|Treating a model's calculated number as objective merely because a spreadsheet produced it.|Methodology, input validation, assumption support, sensitivity analysis, and review challenge.|Reperform calculations and challenge assumptions using relevant independent or historical evidence.|Model risk;Management bias;Valuation
""")

add("FRMC / Risk foundations and governance", "erm", r"""
FRMC|What does FRMC mean?|Here FRMC means Financial Risk Management Controls: the governance, processes, and safeguards used to manage financial exposures.|FRMC is a descriptive term in this project, not one universally mandated global framework. Scope may include liquidity, credit, market, operational, fraud, and reporting risks.|A treasury function combines counterparty limits, cash forecasting, payment authorization, and risk reporting.|Define the organization's FRMC scope, risk taxonomy, owners, and applicable obligations.|Treating FRMC as a single law or assuming another company's control catalog applies unchanged.|Risk taxonomy, governance mandate, control inventory, and applicable-policy register.|Check whether material financial exposures have owners, responses, and monitoring.|Financial risk;Risk governance;Internal control
Financial risk|What is financial risk in simple terms?|Financial risk is exposure to adverse financial outcomes such as inability to pay obligations, counterparty default, or changes in market prices.|Risk categories overlap: an operational failure can cause a cash loss, and liquidity stress can amplify market losses.|A delayed customer payment creates credit exposure and may also strain the company's cash position.|Use consistent categories while tracking connected effects and common causes.|Treating categories as isolated or counting the same exposure repeatedly without reconciliation.|Exposure inventories, cash flows, counterparties, market positions, and risk assessments.|Identify the underlying cause, potential event, financial consequence, and existing responses.|Liquidity risk;Credit risk;Market risk
Inherent risk|What is inherent risk?|Inherent risk is the assessed exposure before considering the specified controls or responses.|The measurement basis must be explicit because methodologies differ in how they treat structural features and existing business conditions.|A high-volume payment process has significant fraud exposure before approval and bank verification controls are considered.|Define the pre-control scenario consistently across assessments.|Calling a risk low because controls are strong while labeling that score inherent risk.|Risk definitions, exposure data, assumptions, and pre-control scenarios.|Challenge whether the rating secretly credits the controls meant to be excluded.|Residual risk;Risk assessment;Control effectiveness
Residual risk|What is residual risk?|Residual risk is the exposure remaining after considering the relevant controls and risk responses.|It is an assessment, not automatically inherent risk minus a control score. Weak evidence or correlated controls can make simple arithmetic misleading.|Dual authorization reduces payment fraud exposure, but collusion and compromised credentials leave residual risk.|Base the assessment on demonstrated control performance and realistic remaining scenarios.|Subtracting ordinal heat-map scores as though they were measured probabilities.|Control results, remaining scenarios, loss data, and documented rating rationale.|Compare remaining exposure with tolerance and challenge untested assumptions about mitigation.|Inherent risk;Risk appetite;Compensating controls
Risk appetite|What is risk appetite?|Risk appetite expresses the types and amount of risk an organization is willing to accept in pursuing its objectives.|It guides choices and needs translation into measures, limits, and escalation that operating teams can use.|A company accepts moderate earnings volatility but has very low appetite for unauthorized payments.|Connect board-level statements with business decisions and actionable thresholds.|Publishing broad slogans that do not influence approvals, limits, or investment choices.|Approved appetite statements, decision records, and linked metrics.|Test whether a proposed activity outside appetite is challenged or escalated.|Risk tolerance;Strategy;Risk governance
Risk tolerance|How is risk tolerance different from risk appetite?|Risk tolerance expresses acceptable variation or boundaries around objectives, while appetite gives broader direction on accepted risk.|Organizations may use terminology differently, so define the relationship among appetite, tolerances, limits, and capacity explicitly.|A liquidity appetite statement is translated into a minimum forecast cash buffer and escalation triggers.|Assign owners and actions to each measurable boundary.|Using appetite, tolerance, and capacity interchangeably without definitions.|Metric definitions, approved limits, breach records, and escalation actions.|Check whether tolerances make the broader appetite operational and remain within capacity.|Risk capacity;KRIs;Risk limits
Risk matrices|How does a risk matrix work?|A risk matrix places risks into categories using defined likelihood and impact scales.|It helps prioritize discussion but is not a precise loss model. Ordinal scores, inconsistent time horizons, and false precision can distort comparisons.|Two risks both rated high are discussed using different causes, exposure amounts, and response options rather than treated as identical.|Define scales, horizon, evidence, and how ratings influence decisions.|Multiplying labels and presenting the result as a scientifically measured probability.|Scoring definitions, risk rationales, calibration examples, and review records.|Check rating consistency and whether important tail risks are hidden by averaging.|Heat maps;Quantitative risk;Inherent risk
Control objectives|What is a control objective?|A control objective states the outcome a control should achieve in addressing a specific risk.|It is different from the activity itself. Clear objectives make design and testing criteria easier to evaluate.|The objective is that only authorized supplier bank changes take effect; an independent callback is one possible activity.|State the desired outcome before selecting the control mechanism.|Writing perform monthly review as the objective without explaining what the review must detect.|Risk statements, objective definitions, and linked control procedures.|Ask whether the described activity can achieve the stated outcome under realistic conditions.|Risk control matrix;Control design;Risk mitigation
Risk mitigation|What are the main ways to respond to risk?|Common responses include avoiding, reducing, sharing or transferring, and accepting risk, with choices suited to the objective and exposure.|Insurance and outsourcing can share consequences but may leave operational, legal, or reputational exposure. Controls rarely eliminate all risk.|A company reduces payment fraud through authorization and transfers part of the financial loss exposure through insurance.|Evaluate response effectiveness, cost, dependencies, and remaining exposure.|Assuming insurance transfers all accountability or a control reduces risk to zero.|Response decisions, contracts, control evidence, and residual-risk analysis.|Check whether the selected response changes the specific cause or consequence that matters.|Residual risk;Insurance;Risk acceptance
Risk acceptance|Who can accept a risk that has not been fully fixed?|An authorized management or governance body can accept risk within its delegated authority and applicable obligations.|Acceptance should be informed, documented, time-bound where appropriate, and escalated when outside tolerance. It cannot waive legal requirements.|A manager temporarily accepts a low-impact process gap with interim safeguards and a dated action plan.|State the exposure, rationale, authority, review date, and conditions for escalation.|Letting internal audit accept management's risk or treating acceptance as permission to break the law.|Acceptance approval, exposure analysis, compensating measures, and review schedule.|Verify authority and whether changed facts invalidate the original acceptance.|Risk appetite;Remediation;Board oversight
Key risk indicators|What are KRIs, and how do they differ from KPIs?|KRIs signal changes in risk exposure; KPIs track performance against objectives. Some measures can serve both purposes depending on use.|A useful KRI has reliable data, a threshold, an owner, and a defined response. A dashboard without action is only reporting.|Increasing overdue receivables may indicate credit deterioration before write-offs occur.|Use leading and lagging indicators and periodically recalibrate thresholds.|Selecting easy-to-measure indicators with no demonstrated connection to the risk.|Metric definitions, source validation, thresholds, trend history, and response logs.|Check whether breaches prompt action and whether indicators provided useful warning.|Risk monitoring;Risk tolerance;Management reporting
Risk reporting|What should a good risk report tell senior management?|It should explain significant exposures, changes, limit breaches, control weaknesses, and decisions or actions needed.|Reports need context about uncertainty and aggregation; a red-amber-green label alone rarely explains the business consequence.|A treasury report shows cash shortfalls under stress, available funding, and the decision deadline.|Separate facts, estimates, assumptions, and management actions.|Hiding overdue actions behind an overall green status or overwhelming leaders with unprioritized metrics.|Reporting packs, data lineage, commentary, decisions, and action follow-up.|Trace a significant reported risk to reliable data and a clear management response.|KRIs;Risk governance;Audit committee
Fraud risk|What is fraud risk?|Fraud risk is exposure to intentional deception or misuse that causes loss, misleading reporting, or other harm.|Assess incentives, opportunities, rationalization, override, and possible collusion; a risk indicator is not proof of misconduct.|A supplier bank change followed by an unusual urgent payment triggers independent verification.|Design controls around credible schemes and protect confidential escalation.|Accusing an employee based only on an anomaly or assuming approvals eliminate collusion.|Fraud scenarios, access and transaction data, reports, and investigation protocols.|Test how a plausible scheme could bypass existing safeguards without presuming guilt.|Fraud triangle;Whistleblowing;Management override
Compliance risk|What is compliance risk?|Compliance risk is exposure from failing to meet applicable laws, regulations, contractual duties, or internal requirements.|Different obligations carry different authority and consequences, so a compliance register should distinguish them and track change.|A business checks whether a new service creates reporting, licensing, or contractual obligations before launch.|Assign obligation owners and link changes to affected processes and controls.|Treating internal policy breaches and statutory violations as legally identical.|Obligation registers, change assessments, control mappings, and compliance reviews.|Trace a selected obligation to current applicability, implemented controls, and monitoring.|Regulatory change;Policy management;Legal risk
Operational risk|How is operational risk different from financial risk?|Operational risk arises from failures involving processes, people, systems, or external events and can produce financial and nonfinancial consequences.|It overlaps with financial risk but describes a different causal perspective. Definitions vary by framework and regulatory context.|A failed payment file creates duplicate disbursements and disrupts supplier relationships.|Capture causes, near misses, dependencies, and recovery actions rather than only realized losses.|Excluding operational incidents from financial-risk discussions because no loss has yet been booked.|Incident records, process maps, near-miss logs, and control assessments.|Check whether recurring incidents reveal systemic weaknesses or concentrated dependencies.|Business continuity;Fraud risk;Loss events
""")

add("FRMC / Treasury and quantitative risk", "bank", r"""
Liquidity risk|What is liquidity risk?|Liquidity risk is the risk of being unable to meet obligations when due without unacceptable cost or loss.|Cash-flow timing, funding access, collateral demands, and market liquidity matter alongside profitability. A profitable company can still run out of cash.|A seasonal business faces payroll before major customers settle invoices.|Maintain realistic forecasts, diversified funding, stress scenarios, and contingency actions.|Using accounting profit as a substitute for a cash-flow forecast.|Cash forecasts, maturity profiles, committed facilities, and stress results.|Challenge timing assumptions, facility availability, and cash needs under adverse scenarios.|Cash forecasting;Funding risk;Contingency funding
Cash forecasting|How should cash forecasts be controlled?|Cash forecast controls support complete inputs, reasonable timing assumptions, review, and comparison with actual cash flows.|The forecast horizon and detail should match decisions, including payroll, debt service, and stressed funding needs.|Treasury updates a 13-week forecast and investigates why collections repeatedly arrive later than assumed.|Back-test forecast accuracy and separate committed cash flows from uncertain estimates.|Rolling forward optimistic assumptions without investigating repeated misses.|Forecast versions, input sources, assumptions, variance analysis, and approvals.|Reconcile starting cash and test significant forecast receipts and payments against support.|Liquidity risk;Scenario analysis;Treasury controls
Market risk|What is market risk?|Market risk is exposure to changes in prices or rates, such as interest rates, foreign exchange, equity prices, or commodity prices.|Measure net and gross exposures, correlations, and nonlinear effects where relevant. Hedging can introduce basis and counterparty risks.|An importer faces higher local-currency costs when the foreign currency strengthens.|Set limits, independently value positions, and assess stressed as well as normal conditions.|Assuming historical correlations or a hedge will hold under every market condition.|Position inventories, valuations, sensitivities, limits, and stress tests.|Reconcile positions and challenge assumptions behind measured exposures.|Foreign exchange;Interest rate risk;Value at Risk
Credit risk|What is credit risk?|Credit risk is the risk that a borrower or counterparty fails to meet contractual obligations.|Assessment considers financial strength, exposure, collateral, concentration, terms, and deterioration over time. A high credit rating is not a guarantee.|A customer exceeds its credit limit while overdue balances continue increasing.|Use approval standards, limits, ongoing monitoring, and documented exceptions.|Approving exposure based only on past payment history or collateral's original value.|Credit files, exposure reports, aging, collateral information, and limit decisions.|Reconcile total exposure and challenge repayment assumptions and concentrations.|Counterparty risk;Expected credit loss;Credit limits
Counterparty concentration|Why does counterparty concentration matter?|Concentration increases vulnerability when exposures share a borrower, group, sector, geography, or other common risk driver.|Several nominally separate counterparties may fail together, so diversification needs economic substance.|A company's deposits are spread across accounts but mostly held within one banking group.|Aggregate related exposures and set limits for meaningful common drivers.|Counting account numbers as independent diversification.|Group mappings, exposure aggregation, concentration limits, and stress scenarios.|Identify common ownership or economic factors hidden by legal-entity labels.|Credit risk;Liquidity risk;Portfolio risk
Interest rate risk|How can interest rate changes create financial risk?|Rate changes can affect borrowing costs, asset values, income, and cash-flow timing.|Fixed and floating exposures behave differently, and repricing mismatches or embedded options may make simple net balances misleading.|A floating-rate loan becomes more expensive while revenue does not rise with rates.|Map repricing dates and assess sensitivity under multiple rate scenarios.|Assuming fixed-rate debt has no market-value sensitivity or all rates move together.|Debt terms, repricing schedules, sensitivities, and hedge documentation.|Check the effect of rate changes on cash flows and valuation separately.|Market risk;Duration;Hedging
Foreign exchange risk|How should a company manage foreign exchange risk?|Identify currency exposures, decide acceptable risk, and use approved operational or financial responses with monitoring.|Transaction, translation, and economic exposures are different. Hedging decisions and hedge accounting eligibility require separate analyses.|An exporter with dollar receivables uses an approved forward contract to reduce home-currency cash-flow uncertainty.|Reconcile forecasts and actual exposures before entering hedges.|Hedging forecast amounts that no longer exist or assuming every economic hedge qualifies for hedge accounting.|Currency exposures, hedge approvals, confirmations, valuations, and reconciliations.|Compare hedge terms with the underlying exposure and investigate over-hedging.|Derivatives;Market risk;Hedge accounting
Value at Risk|What does Value at Risk actually tell us?|VaR estimates a loss threshold for a specified horizon and confidence level under a model's assumptions.|It does not state the worst possible loss or describe the size of losses beyond the threshold. Model choice, data, and changing markets affect reliability.|A one-day 99% VaR of 1 million means the model estimates a 1% probability of a greater one-day loss, not a 1 million maximum loss.|Pair VaR with stress tests, limit monitoring, and back-testing.|Calling VaR the maximum loss or adding portfolio VaRs without considering dependence.|Model methodology, position data, confidence level, horizon, and back-test results.|Challenge tail assumptions, data history, nonlinear exposures, and observed exceedances.|Expected shortfall;Stress testing;Model risk
Expected shortfall|How is expected shortfall different from VaR?|Expected shortfall measures average loss in the modeled tail beyond a specified percentile, while VaR identifies the threshold.|Expected shortfall says more about tail severity but still depends on assumptions and data. It is not a guarantee about future crisis losses.|Two portfolios have similar VaR but one has much larger average losses in severe simulated outcomes.|Use consistent horizons, confidence levels, and validated tail modeling.|Assuming expected shortfall removes model risk or can be compared across incompatible settings.|Loss distributions, methodology, scenario assumptions, and validation reports.|Check whether tail data and modeling support the claimed precision.|Value at Risk;Tail risk;Market risk
Stress testing|What is the difference between stress testing and scenario analysis?|Scenario analysis explores outcomes under specified conditions; stress testing emphasizes severe conditions and their implications for resilience.|Scenarios should be coherent and decision-relevant. Reverse stress testing starts with an unacceptable outcome and asks what could cause it.|Treasury combines delayed collections, reduced credit lines, and collateral calls to test funding resilience.|Include interacting shocks and translate results into actions and triggers.|Choosing only mild scenarios or treating a scenario as a precise forecast.|Scenario rationale, assumptions, calculation results, and management response plans.|Challenge plausibility, severity, dependencies, and whether actions remain feasible under stress.|Liquidity risk;Reverse stress testing;Risk appetite
Model risk|What is model risk and how do we control it?|Model risk is exposure from incorrect models or inappropriate use of their outputs.|Controls address development, data, assumptions, implementation, validation, use limitations, changes, and monitoring. Complexity is not evidence of accuracy.|A credit model underestimates losses after customer behavior changes and is subject to independent review.|Maintain a model inventory and validation proportionate to impact and complexity.|Relying on vendor reputation or high historical accuracy without checking current suitability.|Model documentation, data lineage, validation, change logs, and monitoring.|Compare intended use with actual use and challenge assumptions under changed conditions.|AI governance;Valuation;Back-testing
Expected credit losses|How should expected credit loss estimates be controlled?|Controls should support the applicable accounting model's scope, data, assumptions, calculations, and review of credit-loss estimates.|IFRS 9 and U.S. GAAP CECL have different requirements; do not mix their staging and lifetime-loss mechanics. Forecast uncertainty and management overlays need support.|A lender reviews loan data, scenario assumptions, model output, and separately approved overlays.|Specify the applicable accounting framework and independently challenge significant judgment.|Using IFRS 9 staging rules in a CECL calculation or adding unsupported overlays to reach a target.|Accounting policy, loan data reconciliations, model documentation, forecasts, and approvals.|Assess framework compliance, input reliability, assumption support, and potential management bias.|Credit risk;Accounting estimates;Model risk
Risk limits|What should happen when a financial risk limit is breached?|A breach should trigger validated reporting, prompt escalation, exposure assessment, and an authorized response.|The response depends on severity and policy and may include reducing exposure, restricting activity, or temporary approval within delegated authority.|A dealer exceeds an approved counterparty limit and new trades are restricted pending review.|Define breach owners, deadlines, escalation levels, and repeat-breach consequences.|Raising a limit after the fact to hide the breach or letting traders approve their own exceptions.|Limit configuration, breach logs, notifications, approvals, and corrective trades.|Trace breaches through timely action and assess recurring override patterns.|Risk appetite;KRIs;Trading controls
Business continuity|How do business continuity controls reduce financial risk?|They support continued critical operations and recovery when disruptions threaten payments, reporting, or services.|Plans need realistic dependencies, recovery priorities, people, data, and tested alternatives. A backup alone is not a complete continuity capability.|A treasury team tests how to authorize essential payments if its primary platform is unavailable.|Test recovery scenarios and reconcile data after failover or restoration.|Declaring readiness because a plan exists or a backup job completed.|Business impact analysis, recovery objectives, exercise results, and action tracking.|Check whether tests demonstrate usable recovery within the required business tolerance.|Operational resilience;Backups;Liquidity risk
""")

add("GRC / Governance and assurance", "lines", r"""
Three Lines Model|What are the three lines of defense?|The Three Lines Model describes management's risk ownership and oversight roles and internal audit's independent assurance role, under governing-body oversight.|First-line roles deliver products and services and manage risk; second-line roles provide expertise, support, monitoring, and challenge. Internal audit provides independent assurance and advice. The model concerns roles, not a mandatory three-department chart.|Operations owns payment controls, compliance monitors policy adherence, and internal audit independently evaluates the arrangement.|Define accountabilities and coordinate assurance while protecting internal audit independence.|Making the second line or internal audit the owner of every business risk.|Mandates, reporting lines, role maps, and assurance coordination records.|Check that risk decisions remain with management and independent assurance is not compromised.|Risk ownership;Internal audit independence;Board governance
GRC integration|What does GRC mean in a company?|Governance, risk, and compliance connects decision authority, uncertainty management, and fulfillment of obligations.|A GRC tool can organize records and workflows, but management practices and data quality determine whether the program works.|A company links a regulatory obligation to risks, controls, evidence, and remediation owners in one register.|Use shared definitions and preserve the distinct authority of laws, policies, and risk decisions.|Treating a software purchase as proof of effective governance.|Governance mandates, risk and obligation registers, control links, and issue workflows.|Trace a significant obligation through accountability, controls, evidence, and oversight decisions.|ERM;Compliance risk;Control inventory
""")

add("Internal audit / Planning and professional practice", "iia", r"""
Internal audit|What does internal audit actually do?|Internal audit provides independent, objective assurance and advice about governance, risk management, and controls.|Its scope can extend beyond financial reporting to operations, technology, compliance, and strategy-related risks. Management retains ownership of decisions and controls.|Internal audit evaluates whether procurement safeguards address supplier fraud and reports weaknesses to accountable leaders.|Agree a clear mandate, access rights, and independent reporting arrangements.|Treating internal audit as the department responsible for fixing or operating every control.|Audit charter, approved plan, engagement files, and oversight communications.|Check whether work addresses significant risks and conclusions are supported by evidence.|Three Lines Model;Audit charter;External audit
Internal versus external audit|What is the difference between internal and external audit?|Internal audit serves organizational governance through assurance and advice; external financial-statement audit expresses an independent opinion under its engagement and applicable standards.|Their objectives, scope, reporting audiences, and responsibilities differ, although coordination can reduce unnecessary duplication.|Internal audit evaluates procurement efficiency while the external auditor assesses evidence relevant to financial statements.|Clarify each engagement's objective and coordinate without transferring accountability.|Assuming internal audit can issue the external auditor's statutory opinion.|Engagement mandates, reports, assurance maps, and coordination records.|Compare the purpose and evidence requirements before relying on another assurance provider's work.|Audit opinions;Combined assurance;Independence
Audit charter|What should an internal audit charter cover?|A charter documents internal audit's mandate, authority, responsibilities, position, and relationships with the board and management.|It supports access and independence and should fit applicable professional standards and the organization's governance arrangements.|The charter gives the chief audit executive direct access to the audit committee and necessary records.|Review the charter when organizational responsibilities or governance change.|Keeping a generic charter that does not match actual access or reporting lines.|Approved charter, board records, access arrangements, and role descriptions.|Check whether the function can exercise the authority described in practice.|Chief audit executive;Board oversight;Independence
Audit independence|How can internal audit stay independent while helping management?|Internal audit can advise while avoiding management decision-making and disclosing or safeguarding potential impairments.|Organizational independence and individual objectivity are related but distinct; prior responsibility and conflicts may affect assignment decisions.|An auditor advises on risk considerations for a new process, while management selects and owns the controls.|Document advisory boundaries and arrange independent assurance where prior involvement could impair objectivity.|Designing, approving, operating, and then independently rating the same control without safeguards.|Assignment histories, conflict disclosures, advisory terms, and reporting arrangements.|Assess actual and perceived impairment before assigning assurance work.|Objectivity;Three Lines Model;Consulting engagements
Risk-based audit planning|How do auditors build a risk-based audit plan?|They identify auditable areas, assess significant risks, consider stakeholder needs and prior results, and allocate assurance resources accordingly.|The plan should change when risks change. Repeating a fixed rotation may leave emerging exposures unexamined.|A major ERP migration moves technology and financial close assurance ahead of a routine low-risk audit.|Document priorities, coverage gaps, resources, and the process for updating the plan.|Calling a calendar risk-based without explaining why higher-risk areas receive attention.|Audit universe, risk assessment, stakeholder input, resource plan, and board approval.|Compare planned coverage with the most significant current risks and unaddressed gaps.|Audit universe;Dynamic planning;Risk assessment
Engagement planning|What should be included in an audit engagement plan?|Define objectives, scope, evaluation criteria, risks, procedures, resources, timing, and communication arrangements.|A useful plan makes clear what question the audit will answer and what evidence can support that conclusion.|A vendor audit targets unauthorized bank changes over a defined period and specifies the applicable policy and systems.|Agree the scope and criteria early, then revise when evidence reveals material changes.|Starting fieldwork with a generic checklist and no clear audit objective.|Planning memo, risk assessment, scope, criteria, and work program.|Check whether each procedure contributes evidence toward a defined objective.|Audit work program;Scope limitations;Audit criteria
Audit findings|What makes a well-written audit finding?|A useful finding connects the observed condition with criteria, cause, risk or effect, and a practical management response.|The report should distinguish verified facts from inferred causes and potential consequences, and explain why the issue matters.|Vendor changes bypassed required verification, increasing exposure to redirected payments; the cause was an uncontrolled workflow path.|Validate facts with the process owner and retain evidence supporting scope and impact.|Writing vague findings such as controls need improvement without explaining the failure and consequence.|Criteria, tested exceptions, exposure analysis, root cause support, and management response.|Ask whether a reader can understand what failed, why it matters, and what must change.|Root cause;Recommendations;Audit reporting
Audit recommendations|How do auditors make practical recommendations?|Recommendations should address the cause and risk of a finding and be feasible in the organization's context.|Management owns the action plan. An auditor can suggest outcomes or options without assuming responsibility for implementation.|Rather than add another signature, management removes an unauthorized vendor-change path and verifies new routing.|Agree accountable owners, measurable actions, and realistic dates tied to exposure.|Recommending more training when system design makes bypass easy.|Finding analysis, proposed actions, management agreements, and closure criteria.|Check whether the action changes the condition that allowed the failure.|CAPA;Root cause;Management action plans
Root cause analysis|How do I find the root cause of an audit issue?|Investigate the underlying conditions that allowed the issue, using evidence and techniques such as causal mapping or repeated why questions.|Human error may describe the event without explaining workload, design, incentives, training, or supervision that made it likely.|Repeated late reviews trace to a report arriving after the required deadline rather than reviewer forgetfulness.|Validate causes against multiple instances and distinguish symptoms from contributing factors.|Stopping at employee error or forcing every issue into one simple cause.|Process timelines, interviews, incident patterns, and corroborating records.|Check whether removing the proposed cause would plausibly prevent recurrence.|CAPA;Process design;Recurring findings
CAPA|What is CAPA in an audit context?|Corrective and preventive action addresses identified problems and reduces the chance of recurrence or similar failures.|Terminology varies by organization; the practical requirements are clear ownership, cause-based actions, dates, and validation of effectiveness.|A team corrects duplicate payments, fixes the matching logic, and monitors for recurrence.|Separate immediate correction from sustainable process improvement.|Closing CAPA when the affected transaction is corrected but the cause remains.|Action plans, implementation records, effectiveness checks, and recurrence monitoring.|Evaluate whether the action resolved the actual cause and whether similar processes are exposed.|Remediation;Root cause;Issue closure
Audit reports|What should a clear internal audit report contain?|A report should communicate the objective and scope, supported conclusions, significant findings, and agreed actions with owners and timing.|Disclose material limitations and explain rating criteria. The level of detail should help the intended audience make decisions.|An audit committee summary highlights payment fraud exposure while an appendix gives management the specific workflow defects.|Lead with business significance and preserve traceability to evidence.|Using alarming language unsupported by the work or burying the required decision in technical detail.|Final report, reviewed workpapers, rating methodology, and management responses.|Check that every significant conclusion is supported and that scope limits are visible.|Audit findings;Executive communication;Assurance conclusions
Audit follow-up|How do internal auditors verify that findings are closed?|They assess whether management implemented agreed actions and whether evidence supports effective risk reduction.|Follow-up depth depends on risk and the nature of the action. Management confirmation alone may be insufficient for significant issues.|An auditor inspects and tests a redesigned vendor-change workflow instead of accepting a screenshot of the new policy.|Set closure criteria when issuing the finding and escalate overdue high-risk actions.|Closing findings automatically at the target date or accepting unsupported status updates.|Action records, operating evidence, retests, and closure approvals.|Check that the fix operates and that residual exposure is acceptable or properly escalated.|CAPA;Risk acceptance;Remediation
Audit scope limitations|What should an auditor do when evidence or access is restricted?|Assess how the restriction affects the engagement, seek resolution, document alternatives, and communicate significant limitations to appropriate oversight.|A limitation can prevent a supported conclusion; the auditor should not replace missing evidence with unwarranted certainty.|Management denies access to key payment logs, so internal audit escalates the restriction and qualifies the scope of its conclusion.|Record what was unavailable, why it mattered, and the effect on assurance.|Issuing an unqualified positive conclusion over areas that could not be examined.|Access requests, responses, alternative procedures, and oversight communications.|Determine which objectives remain unsupported despite alternative work.|Audit evidence;Independence;Board oversight
Professional skepticism|What does professional skepticism look like in practice?|It means questioning evidence appropriately, considering contradictions, and avoiding unsupported assumptions while remaining fair.|Skepticism is not an accusation of dishonesty. It requires assessing both corroborating and contrary information.|An auditor investigates why approval timestamps follow payment dates rather than accepting a general statement that approvals always occur first.|Resolve inconsistencies and document the reasoning behind significant judgments.|Treating management explanations as proof or assuming every anomaly is fraud.|Contradictory records, follow-up procedures, corroboration, and judgment notes.|Check whether a plausible alternative explanation was considered and supported.|Audit evidence;Fraud risk;Professional judgment
Audit quality assurance|How is the quality of an internal audit function evaluated?|A quality program assesses conformance with applicable standards and the function's effectiveness, using ongoing and periodic evaluation.|The IIA Global Internal Audit Standards became effective January 9, 2025. Under those standards, an external quality assessment is required at least once every five years, subject to the specified arrangements.|A function reviews engagement quality continuously and schedules an independent external assessment.|Track quality findings to improvement actions and use current applicable standards.|Assuming completion of the annual audit plan proves quality or using obsolete standards without analysis.|Quality program, internal assessments, external assessment records, and improvement plans.|Check both standards conformance and whether the function delivers useful, supported assurance.|Global Internal Audit Standards;External quality assessment;Audit supervision
""")

add("Internal audit / Evidence and testing", "evidence", r"""
Audit evidence|What counts as good audit evidence?|Good evidence is sufficient in quantity and appropriate in relevance and reliability for the conclusion being drawn.|More weak evidence does not necessarily compensate for poor quality. Source, authenticity, completeness, timing, and how information was obtained all matter.|A dated approval log tied to the actual transaction supports authorization better than an undated statement that approvals normally happen.|Specify the assertion and period each item supports and investigate contradictory evidence.|Treating a screenshot or verbal statement as conclusive without context.|Source records, provenance, population support, timestamps, and corroboration.|Ask whether the evidence supports the exact conclusion rather than a related but different claim.|Sufficiency;Reliability;Audit documentation
Audit assertions|What are financial statement assertions?|Assertions are representations about transactions, balances, and disclosures that auditors use to identify possible misstatements.|Relevant concepts include existence or occurrence, completeness, rights and obligations, valuation or allocation, and presentation and disclosure; applicable standards may organize terms differently.|Inspecting recorded inventory supports existence, while tracing warehouse items into records addresses completeness.|Map procedures to the assertion and choose the direction of testing deliberately.|Assuming a single procedure proves every assertion about a balance.|Assertion mapping, selected records, supporting documents, and procedure results.|Check whether the test direction and evidence genuinely address the specified misstatement risk.|Existence;Completeness;Valuation
Tests of controls versus substantive procedures|What is the difference between control testing and substantive testing?|Control testing evaluates whether safeguards are designed or operate effectively; substantive procedures seek evidence about misstatements in transactions, balances, or disclosures.|A procedure can have dual purposes if designed accordingly, but evidence of a correct balance alone does not prove the control operated.|Inspecting review evidence tests a reconciliation control; independently verifying the bank balance provides substantive evidence.|State the objective of each procedure and evaluate each conclusion separately.|Assuming no errors found means the control must have worked.|Test objectives, control attributes, substantive support, and separate conclusions.|Determine whether the work supports process reliability, reported amounts, or both.|Operating effectiveness;Audit assertions;Dual-purpose tests
Inquiry and reperformance|Is asking a control owner enough to test a control?|Inquiry helps understand a control, but inquiry alone does not provide sufficient support for an operating-effectiveness conclusion.|Inspection, observation, and reperformance can corroborate what people describe. Observation itself may only show performance at one time.|A reviewer explains variance investigation, and the auditor inspects actual explanations and independently recalculates selected variances.|Combine procedures according to risk and the evidence each can provide.|Recording the owner's statement as if it were verified operation throughout the year.|Interview notes, actual execution records, observed activity, and reperformance results.|Check whether corroboration supports both the claimed action and relevant period.|Walkthroughs;Audit evidence;Operating effectiveness
Population completeness|Why do auditors validate the testing population?|A sample can only support conclusions about the population it was drawn from, so missing or incorrect items can undermine the test.|Validate scope, dates, systems, duplicates, exclusions, and reconciliation to an independent source where feasible.|A change-management extract excludes direct database changes, leaving the riskiest deployments outside sampling.|Reconcile population totals and inspect extraction logic before selecting items.|Choosing a statistically valid sample from an incomplete report.|Query logic, parameters, reconciliation, inclusion rules, and population totals.|Check whether known items and unusual processing paths appear in the population.|Sampling;IPE;Data analytics
Missing control evidence|Does missing evidence automatically mean a control failed?|Missing evidence means the claimed performance is not yet adequately supported; further work may distinguish a documentation gap from nonperformance.|Contemporaneous corroboration can sometimes help, but reconstructed narratives should not be presented as original execution evidence.|A lost approval attachment is investigated using immutable workflow logs that identify the original decision.|Preserve original records and label any reconstruction with its source and limitations.|Backdating approvals or assuming the owner remembers correctly without corroboration.|Original logs, retention information, corroborating records, and documented limitations.|Determine what can actually be concluded about the control and period from reliable evidence.|Evidence retention;Operating effectiveness;Scope limitations
Audit workpapers|What should an audit workpaper document?|It should show the objective, population, selection, procedure, evidence, results, exceptions, and supported conclusion, with appropriate preparation and review records.|Another qualified reviewer should be able to understand the work without relying on undocumented explanations. Retention rules depend on engagement and jurisdiction.|A workpaper links each sampled vendor change to approval evidence and explains why two deviations affect the conclusion.|Use clear references, preserve evidence provenance, and resolve review comments.|Writing tested and passed without recording what was tested or why it passed.|Work program, indexed evidence, exception analysis, and review sign-offs.|Check whether the conclusion can be reproduced from the retained evidence and documented procedure.|Documentation;Audit supervision;Record retention
Audit analytics|Can data analytics replace audit sampling?|Analytics can examine entire available datasets for defined conditions, but coverage does not guarantee valid data, correct logic, or resolution of anomalies.|Full-population checks reduce selection risk for tested attributes but leave data quality, algorithm, and interpretation risks.|An auditor checks all invoices for exact duplicates, then investigates near-duplicates and valid recurring payments separately.|Validate data and logic using known positive and negative cases and investigate exceptions.|Calling a zero-exception query a clean audit without checking omitted data or detection limits.|Data reconciliations, query versions, validation cases, and investigated outputs.|Assess whether the analytics detect the intended risk and what errors remain outside their design.|Population completeness;Continuous auditing;False positives
""")

add("Internal audit / Sampling", "sampling", r"""
Audit sampling|How do auditors decide which items to sample?|They define the objective and population, choose a suitable selection approach, and set sample extent based on risk and the intended conclusion.|Statistical and nonstatistical approaches require sound judgment. Targeted high-risk items do not automatically support conclusions about the whole population.|An auditor selects representative monthly control executions and separately examines unusual overrides.|Document the population, selection method, expected deviations, and evaluation criteria.|Picking only convenient items or treating targeted selections as a representative statistical sample.|Population validation, sampling rationale, selected identifiers, and results.|Check whether selection and evaluation support the stated population-level conclusion.|Sampling risk;Population completeness;Control testing
Control sample sizes|Is 25 the standard SOX sample size?|No. There is no universal SOX sample size of 25.|Extent depends on the objective, population, frequency, assessed risk, expected deviations, desired assurance, and methodology. Statistical assumptions must match the actual selection and evaluation.|A quarterly control has only four executions, while a daily control has a very different population and risk profile.|Use a justified methodology and explain departures or changes.|Applying a sample-size table without understanding its assumptions.|Sampling methodology, risk assessment, population count, and approved rationale.|Evaluate whether the planned evidence is sufficient for the risk and conclusion sought.|Audit sampling;Frequency;Operating effectiveness
Sampling exceptions|What should we do when a sampled control fails?|Investigate the deviation's nature, cause, timing, and possible extent, then reassess reliance and the need for additional work.|More samples do not erase the original failure. Consider whether the issue is isolated, systemic, or linked to a design problem.|A late approval leads to checking whether approvals routinely follow payments during staff absences.|Keep the failed item in the results and revise the conclusion transparently.|Replacing a failed sample with a passing one or expanding until the percentage looks acceptable.|Original exception, cause analysis, additional procedures, and revised assessment.|Assess implications for the population and the control objective rather than only the observed rate.|Deviation rate;Deficiencies;Remediation
""")

add("Cases / Documented history", "worldcom", r"""
WorldCom case|What can auditors learn from WorldCom?|WorldCom illustrates how improper accounting can materially distort earnings and why unsupported entries and escalation deserve scrutiny.|The SEC's amended complaint described improper accounting and significant income overstatement. The control lessons here are analytical interpretations, not claims that a particular control would certainly have prevented the fraud.|Historical case: operating costs were improperly capitalized; an illustrative audit response is to challenge capitalization support and unusual period-end entries.|Distinguish source-documented facts from inferred lessons and examine accounting substance.|Presenting allegations as final findings without checking case status or claiming one approval would guarantee prevention.|The cited SEC record, accounting support, and separately labeled instructional analysis.|Ask how an unsupported capitalization entry could enter and survive the reporting process.|Journal entries;Capitalization;Professional skepticism
""")
add("Cases / Documented history", "enron", r"""
Enron case|What internal control lessons does Enron offer?|Enron highlights the importance of transparent accounting, independent challenge, conflicts oversight, and scrutiny of complex arrangements.|The cited historical SEC reporting discusses enforcement involving Enron and related arrangements. Modern control recommendations are interpretations of the case rather than quoted historical findings.|Historical context: complex entity arrangements obscured financial exposure; an illustrative response is independent review of consolidation judgments and related-party relationships.|Investigate economic substance and incentives and escalate unresolved accounting concerns.|Reducing the case to one missing control or assuming formal approvals establish transparent reporting.|Historical source records, transaction analyses, conflict disclosures, and review challenge.|Check whether decision-makers received complete information about relationships, obligations, and accounting judgments.|Related parties;Consolidation;Governance
""")
add("Emerging practice / AI and automation", "ai", r"""
AI governance|Can we use AI to test controls or write audit conclusions?|AI can assist with analysis and drafting, but accountable professionals must validate evidence, reasoning, confidentiality, and conclusions.|Generated text is not proof that a control operated. Model errors, unsuitable data, prompt injection, and automation bias can undermine an apparently polished result.|An AI tool flags unusual journals, while an auditor validates the data and investigates supporting documents before reporting a finding.|Define approved uses, protect sensitive information, test performance, and retain human review and provenance.|Uploading unrestricted evidence to unapproved services or treating an AI confidence score as audit assurance.|Use-case approvals, data controls, evaluation results, source evidence, and human review records.|Test realistic errors and adversarial inputs and verify that unsupported questions are not answered confidently.|Model risk;Audit analytics;Evidence provenance
""")

SOURCES.update({
    "soc": ("AICPA SOC suite and engagement resources", "https://www.aicpa-cima.com/resources/landing/system-and-organization-controls-soc-suite-of-services"),
    "ifrs9": ("IFRS Foundation: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments", "https://www.ifrs.org/issued-standards/list-of-standards/ifrs-9-financial-instruments/"),
    "cecl": ("FASB Staff Q&A on Topic 326 expected credit loss estimates", "https://fasb.org/page/PageContent?pageId=%2Fprojects%2Fother-staff-projects%2Ffasb-staff-qatopic-326-no-2developing-an-estimate-of-expect.html"),
})

# Curated real-user aliases supplement the systematic phrasing families below.
# All five SOX phrasings in the request deliberately target one canonical answer.
ALIASES = {
    "SOX": ["Explain Sarbanes Oxley.", "Can you explain SOX in simple words?",
            "What does SOX compliance mean?", "What is the purpose of SOX?", "What is Sarbanes-Oxley?"],
    "SOX objectives": ["Why is SOX important?", "What are the objectives of SOX compliance?"],
    "Section 302": ["What is section 302?", "Explain SOX 302.", "Who signs SOX certifications?"],
    "Section 404": ["Explain Section 404.", "What is 404(a) versus 404(b)?"],
    "Section 409": ["Explain SOX 409.", "Does SOX require real-time disclosures?"],
    "Section 802": ["Explain SOX 802.", "Is every SOX record kept for seven years?"],
    "Section 906": ["Explain SOX 906.", "Compare Section 302 and Section 906."],
    "SOX applicability": ["Who must comply with SOX?", "Does SOX apply to private companies?"],
    "SOX audit lifecycle": ["How does SOX work?", "How do I prepare for a SOX audit?", "What is the SOX compliance process?"],
    "SOX and COSO comparison": ["Difference between SOX and COSO?", "SOX vs COSO", "Is COSO the same as SOX?"],
    "COSO versus COBIT": ["Difference between COSO and COBIT.", "COSO vs COBIT"],
    "COSO versus ISO 31000": ["COSO vs ISO 31000", "Can a company be ISO 31000 certified?"],
    "COSO five components": ["What are the five COSO components?", "Name the 5 COSO pillars."],
    "COSO 17 principles": ["Explain the 17 COSO principles", "What are the seventeen COSO principles?"],
    "COSO implementation": ["How do companies implement COSO?"],
    "ICFR": ["What is ICFR?", "Explain financial reporting controls.", "What are internal controls over financial reporting?"],
    "Control design": ["What is design effectiveness?", "How is control design different from operating effectiveness?"],
    "Operating effectiveness": ["How do I know a control really operated?"],
    "IT general controls": ["What are ITGCs?", "Explain IT general controls.", "What is ITGC?"],
    "Segregation of duties": ["What is SoD?", "What is separation of duties?"],
    "Audit walkthroughs": ["What is an audit walkthrough?", "What is a walkthrough?"],
    "Audit evidence": ["What is evidence?", "What is audit evidence?"],
    "Tests of controls versus substantive procedures": ["How do auditors test controls?", "How is testing performed?", "How do you test controls and evidence?"],
    "Material weaknesses": ["What is a material weakness?", "Does every control failure mean a material weakness?"],
    "Significant deficiencies": ["What is a significant deficiency?"],
    "Control deficiencies": ["What is a control deficiency?"],
    "Key controls": ["What are key controls?"],
    "Preventive controls": ["What are preventive controls?"],
    "Detective controls": ["What are detective controls?"],
    "COSO risk assessment": ["Explain risk assessment.", "How do I assess risk under COSO?"],
    "Entity-level controls": ["What is an entity-level control?", "Explain ELCs."],
    "Risk matrices": ["How do risk matrices work?", "What is a risk heat map?"],
    "Three Lines Model": ["What is the three lines model?", "Explain the three lines of defence."],
    "Information produced by the entity": ["What is IPE?", "How do we check report completeness and accuracy?"],
    "SOX misconceptions": ["Does SOX require approval above 500000?", "Does COSO require a 500000 approval threshold?"],
}

INTENTS = ("overview", "implementation", "evidence", "assessment", "mistakes", "example", "interview", "expert")


def slug(value):
    return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", "-", value.lower()).strip("-")


def normalize(value):
    return " ".join(re.findall(r"[a-z0-9]+", value.lower()))


def intent_content(t, intent):
    name = t["label"]
    patterns = {
        "overview": (t["question"],
                     [f"Explain {name} in simple terms.", f"Help me understand {name}.", f"Give me an overview of {name}."],
                     t["direct"], t["detail"]),
        "implementation": (f"Where should my team start with {name}?",
                           [f"What are the practical first steps for {name}?", f"How should we approach {name} at work?", f"What should we put in place for {name}?"],
                           t["practice"], f"Start from the relevant objective and decision, then apply this topic-specific guidance: {t['detail']} Define who supplies the information, who makes the decision, and how unresolved issues reach that person. Use these records to make the approach reviewable: {t['evidence']}"),
        "evidence": (f"What evidence should I look for when reviewing {name}?",
                     [f"What documents would support a review of {name}?", f"What should I request as evidence for {name}?", f"What records do I need to assess {name}?"],
                     f"Look for the following, tailored to your review objective: {t['evidence']}",
                     f"Use those records to address this specific question: {t['assess']} Identify the entity, period, source, and version so evidence from a different process or date is not mistaken for support. {t['detail']}"),
        "assessment": (f"How do I assess whether our approach to {name} is sound?",
                       [f"How should I evaluate {name}?", f"What would an auditor check about {name}?", f"How can we validate our approach to {name}?"],
                       t["assess"], f"Build the assessment around the following evidence: {t['evidence']} Record what the information supports, any contradictory results, and the limits of your conclusion. {t['detail']}"),
        "mistakes": (f"What do people commonly get wrong about {name}?",
                     [f"What mistakes should I avoid with {name}?", f"What is a common misconception about {name}?", f"What are the main pitfalls with {name}?"],
                     f"A common mistake is {t['mistake'][0].lower() + t['mistake'][1:]}",
                     f"The more defensible approach is: {t['practice']} The reason is that {t['direct'][0].lower() + t['direct'][1:]} To check whether the misconception has affected your work, use this challenge: {t['assess']}"),
        "example": (f"Can you give me a practical example of {name}?",
                    [f"Show me how {name} works in a real situation.", f"Walk me through an example involving {name}.", f"What would {name} look like in a company?"],
                    t["example"], f"In this example, apply the following assessment rather than assume success from the described activity: {t['assess']} The records needed to substantiate the analysis would include: {t['evidence']} {t['detail']}"),
        "interview": (f"How should I explain {name} in an audit interview?",
                      [f"Give me an interview answer about {name}.", f"How can I discuss {name} with an audit hiring manager?", f"What should I mention if an interviewer asks about {name}?"],
                      t["direct"], f"Explain the mechanism or boundary: {t['detail']} Then describe how you would assess it: {t['assess']} Use the example below as a hypothetical unless it matches your own experience, and explain what evidence would support your conclusion."),
        "expert": (f"How should we challenge our conclusions about {name} in a complex organization?",
                   [f"What expert judgment is needed when assessing {name}?", f"How do we stress-test our reasoning about {name}?", f"What should a senior reviewer challenge about {name}?"],
                   t["assess"], f"Start with the topic's specific limitation: {t['detail']} Challenge whether the supporting information remains valid across relevant entities, systems, and time periods. In particular, avoid this failure: {t['mistake']} Identify contradictory evidence and dependencies before extending a local result to the organization. Document unresolved uncertainty and the decision-maker responsible for it."),
    }
    return patterns[intent]


def topic_sources(t):
    keys = [t["source"]]
    name = t["label"]
    if name in {"Section 302", "Section 404", "Management responsibilities", "Disclosure controls", "ICFR"}:
        keys.append("sec")
    if name in {"Service organization reports", "Complementary user entity controls", "Cloud controls"}:
        keys = ["soc", "icfr"]
    if name in {"Credit risk", "Counterparty concentration"}:
        keys = ["credit"]
    if name == "Expected credit losses":
        keys = ["ifrs9", "cecl"]
    if name in {"Information produced by the entity", "Audit assertions"}:
        keys = ["evidence"]
    return [{"title": SOURCES[key][0], "url": SOURCES[key][1],
             "scope": "Background authority or framework context; examples, suggested evidence and assessment methods are original illustrative guidance, not quoted requirements."}
            for key in dict.fromkeys(keys)]


def build():
    entries = []
    for t in TOPICS:
        topic_id = slug(t["label"])
        for intent in INTENTS:
            question, alternates, direct, detail = intent_content(t, intent)
            if intent == "overview":
                alternates += ALIASES.get(t["label"], [])
            # Keep original phrasing while removing punctuation-only duplicates locally.
            seen = {normalize(question)}
            unique_alternates = []
            for alternate in alternates:
                if normalize(alternate) not in seen:
                    seen.add(normalize(alternate))
                    unique_alternates.append(alternate)
            difficulty = ("Beginner" if intent in {"overview", "example"} else
                          "Advanced" if intent in {"assessment", "expert"} else "Intermediate")
            follow_intents = [x for x in ("overview", "implementation", "assessment", "mistakes") if x != intent][:3]
            entry = {
                "id": f"{topic_id}.{intent}", "topic_id": topic_id,
                "category": t["category"], "question": question,
                "alternate_questions": unique_alternates,
                "answer": f"Direct answer: {direct}\n\nDetailed explanation: {detail}\n\nPractical example: {t['example']}\n\nBest practices: {t['practice']}\n\nCommon mistakes: {t['mistake']}\n\nRelated concepts: {'; '.join(t['related'])}.",
                "direct_answer": direct, "detailed_explanation": detail,
                "example": t["example"], "best_practices": [t["practice"]],
                "common_mistakes": [t["mistake"]], "related_topics": t["related"],
                "difficulty": difficulty, "proficiency": "Expert" if intent == "expert" else difficulty,
                "keywords": list(dict.fromkeys([t["label"], intent, t["category"].split(" / ")[0]] + t["related"])),
                "intent": intent, "is_faq": intent in {"overview", "mistakes"},
                "follow_up_questions": [intent_content(t, x)[0] for x in follow_intents],
                "follow_up_ids": [f"{topic_id}.{x}" for x in follow_intents],
                "sources": topic_sources(t), "reviewed_on": "2026-09-09",
                "review_status": "AI-authored; automated validation; independent domain review pending",
                "jurisdiction": "United States; issuer- and provision-specific" if t["category"].startswith("SOX") else "General guidance; local requirements and engagement standards may differ",
                "example_type": "documented historical context with illustrative analysis" if t["category"].startswith("Cases") else "hypothetical",
            }
            entries.append(entry)
    # Fail on cross-intent or cross-topic alias collisions; never silently change routing.
    routing = {}
    for e in entries:
        for q in [e["question"], *e["alternate_questions"]]:
            key = normalize(q)
            if key in routing and routing[key] != e["id"]:
                raise ValueError(f"Ambiguous alias {q!r}: {routing[key]} and {e['id']}")
            routing[key] = e["id"]
    content = json.dumps(entries, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + "\n"
    (ROOT / "fundamentals.json").write_text(content, encoding="utf-8")
    manifest = {
        "schema_version": "2.0.0", "generated_on": "2026-09-09",
        "canonical_questions": len(entries), "topics": len(TOPICS),
        "alternate_questions": sum(len(e["alternate_questions"]) for e in entries),
        "faq_questions": sum(e["is_faq"] for e in entries),
        "expert_questions": sum(e["proficiency"] == "Expert" for e in entries),
        "categories": dict(sorted(Counter(e["category"] for e in entries).items())),
        "difficulty_counts": dict(Counter(e["difficulty"] for e in entries)),
        "intent_counts": dict(Counter(e["intent"] for e in entries)),
        "sha256": hashlib.sha256(content.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest(),
        "authority_sources": [{"id": key, "title": value[0], "url": value[1]} for key, value in SOURCES.items()],
        "content_policy": {
            "scope": "Broad curated knowledge base, not exhaustive coverage of every possible question or jurisdiction.",
            "authorship": "Original AI-authored educational explanations and practitioner examples; not licensed framework text.",
            "review": "Primary-source checks and automated technical validation do not constitute independent professional sign-off. Domain review is required before reliance in enterprise decisions.",
            "refresh": "Review authority changes before each applicable reporting cycle and after relevant rule or standards updates; reviewed_on is not a guarantee of continuing currency.",
            "version_notes": ["SOX original enacted text is historical; use current implementing rules and amendments for applicability.",
                              "PCAOB AS 2201 page identifies amendments effective December 15, 2026; do not apply future amendments prematurely.",
                              "SEC Release 33-11419 is a proposal, not treated here as effective law.",
                              "IIA Three Lines source reflects the July 2026 statements of position.",
                              "IIA Global Internal Audit Standards effective January 9, 2025 are the standards baseline.",
                              "Expert content uses difficulty Advanced plus proficiency Expert to preserve the requested difficulty enum."],
            "retrieval": "Explicit aliases map to one canonical answer. Unlisted wording uses conservative offline lexical matching, not unrestricted semantic understanding.",
            "examples": "All examples are hypothetical except explicitly labeled historical context. No real client data is included.",
        },
    }
    (ROOT / "manifest.json").write_text(json.dumps(manifest, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    print(json.dumps({k: manifest[k] for k in ("canonical_questions", "topics", "alternate_questions", "faq_questions", "expert_questions")}, indent=2))


if __name__ == "__main__":
    build()
